#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060563} :activity/id #uuid "a629931e-b4d2-5295-b978-709da1971402" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049944 :topic/id #uuid "204f44c0-5b46-57c1-a87d-a8ddbcbb2008" :topic/math-academy-id 226} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060906 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the equation\n\n$$\n\\log_3 x = 4\n$$\n\nCan we determine $x$?\n\nRecall that the inverse operation of the logarithm is exponentiation. So, to isolate $x$, we must exponentiate both sides of this equation.\n\nThe base of the logarithm is $3$, so we use a base of $3$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{3} x &= 4 \\\\\n3^{(\\log_{3} x)} &= 3^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above may look complicated, but since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that\n\n$$\n3^{(\\log_3{x})}=x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 3^{4} \\\\\nx &= 81\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $x = 81$ is the solution." :tutorial/id #uuid "bbfe9e19-d1ed-5df9-97f9-0e9c7f9f50e9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2810 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "eb3435ba-447f-5bc4-baf9-4cf14118da95" :step/math-academy-id 14724 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060718}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059695 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059518 :question/id #uuid "3590f0e0-2ff9-58f4-8351-772b477c1cd2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4109"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98fb5d45-5215-506c-8b2a-99aaadb6631c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234411}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations With Linear Arguments"} :step/id #uuid "cc5be384-ef18-597e-a346-067e1c1259b7" :step/math-academ